WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.000
Today we are going to fight, we're gonna stand
strong for what we believe in: each other.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.100
Last month the corridors of power in Washington
opened up to the world's oldest profession.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.780
40 sex workers met with 30 members of Congress
to push back

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.220
against the Fight Online Sex Trafficking Act.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.280
Why would professional lawmakers meet with
professional law breakers?

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.900
Reason tagged along to find out why.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.300
So what we're doing right now is super exciting
because, as far as we know,

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.860
there has never been a sex worker lobby day
on the hill, but there is today.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.240
We're going to be going to our representatives,
talking with them.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.500
A lot of them aren't super familiar with sex
worker rights.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.620
Particularly in the wake of SESTA and how
SESTA has impacted us.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.860
SESTA is a bill that passed recently

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:03.520
that heavily restricts and criminalizes advertising
for sex work on the internet.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.120
The Stop Enabling Sex Traffickers Act or SESTA

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.820
would allow sex trafficking victims to have
their day in court

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:17.140
by eliminating federal liability protections
for technology providers that knowingly, knowingly

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.800
facilitate online sex trafficking.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.780
Today when the house votes on FOSTA, we will
be sending a clear message

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:31.160
businesses that sell human beings online can
no longer do so with impunity.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.300
FOSTA stands for Fighting Online Sex Trafficking
Act.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:40.280
It is a law that purports to fight minors
being forced into sex work

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:45.760
and it purports to do it by increasing liability
for online intermediaries.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.480
The bill also provides increased criminal
liability and thus deters websites and individuals

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.280
from selling human beings online.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.340
Websites will no longer be able to turn a
blind eye

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.400
or actively conceal this horrific practice

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.300
without facing very real consequences.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.320
The federal government says if you're an online
platform,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:14.460
you generally are not liable for what goes
on, by users, on your site.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:22.180
FOSTA and SESTA before that are the first
steps towards enforcing more liability

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.280
allegedly to go after sex traffickers.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.260
We're really fooling around in an odd way that you
don't expect.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.220
We're fooling around with one of the legal
foundations of the internet

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.790
that's been really so successful particularly
with social media.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:40.450
Cragislist and Reddit have already shuttered
sections of their website

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.880
citing FOSTA liability concerns.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.700
Facebook, and other large technology companies
have backed the legislation

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.810
while a trade group representing startups
has opposed it.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:54.520
Thank you very much for being with us today
as we sign this crucial legislation

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.520
to combat online sex trafficking and bring
criminals to justice.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.940
Signing the bill was very much in character
for President Trump.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:09.080
It's a bill in which it appeals to people's
gut instincts about things.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.020
It doesn't ask questions about are we going
about this in the right way,

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.900
what are the costs, you know all of those
issues, populists don't ask those questions.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.980
Thank you mister President.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.080
The only surprising thing is he didn't support
it more strongly

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:29.840
and I think that's probably because he himself
has legal and indeed political issues

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.540
connected to various kinds of sexual issues
let's say.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.040
Today Carl Ferrer, the CEO of Backpage.com

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.200
the man behind what authorities are calling
one of the world's top online brothels

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.340
facing criminal charges for the very first
time.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:51.060
The purpose of government is not to make people
into better people morally.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:56.960
If government's role is to enforce a majority
or a minority's view

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.320
you're going to be coercing people who are
pursuing their idea of what the good life is.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:15.880
To even discuss sex work is something that
we weren't sure what the response would be for that.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:20.720
And that is the problem that we're trying
to overcome here right now

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.259
is the lack of communication because we've
found that most people

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.000
don't even know what FOSTA and SESTA are slash
were.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.939
And that they were very effectively manipulated
essentially

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.560
into believing that it was truly an anti-trafficking
law

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:46.060
when in reality it is hurting survivors of
trafficking and itr's really hurting sex workers.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:52.220
Sex workers often advertise on the internet
as a way to screen clients.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.660
And this is a huge help in keeping people
safe.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.540
If you don't have the ability

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:01.540
to check on someone's criminal record for
example before meeting up with them

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:03.600
you don't know if they could be violent or
not.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.960
And because FOSTA limits in a lot of ways
the ways I am able to advertise and run my business

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.060
it makes it more dangerous for me.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.080
The Department of Justice had sent over a
letter, very last minute

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.600
kind of sticking to the, I don't know if I'd
call it ad hoc,

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.800
but the fast moving nature of the bill

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.540
that they obviously weren't even consulted
before it was brought to the floor.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:34.080
And even DOJ was saying that they question
highly the constitutionality.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.940
We live in a system where the process is supposed
to be deliberative, it's supposed to be fairly slow,

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.800
you're supposed to have committee work, it's
supposed to be informed and detailed

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.520
the original law had many problems and there
was a lot of informed criticism of it

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.240
and Congress on the house side was responding

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.649
but then suddenly it was pulled out of committee
on the house side

00:05:54.649 --> 00:06:00.580
brought to the floor for a vote and passed
and then signed by President Trump.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.280
Once it got out of that and got defines as
'are you for or against

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.880
coercively forcing children to engage in sex.'

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.060
You know, that was the definition and no one
was gonna vote for that.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:20.679
These are tough bills because you, the titles
of them and what people say that they do-

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:24.490
it's a set up, because then it's going to
be an election year

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:28.809
and it's like 'well this person voted for
child slavery.'

00:06:28.809 --> 00:06:29.900
He was great!

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.040
-find a meeting that goes better than that
one!

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.240
I mean he talked to us for an hour-

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.760
I don't often leave a building feeling hopeful
for my government.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:45.040
Exactly, yeah feeling like 'wow I feel like
I'm being represented by my elected officials.'

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.720
Over 30 more congressional representatives
would open their doors to Jinx

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.940
and other sex worker activists that day.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.039
And by the end of the month, the Electronic
Frontier Foundation

00:06:54.039 --> 00:06:57.369
joined Human Rights Watch and the Woodhull
Freedom Foundation

00:06:57.369 --> 00:07:00.300
in a lawsuit challenging FOSTA in federal
court.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.900
What do we do?

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.520
Stand up, fight back!

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.140
I'm not saying I'm happy with Washington D.C.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.400
I''m not saying I'm happy with the politicians.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.640
I'm glad they did the thing that they are
supposed to do, which is represent us.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.680
So you can't bring down the whole system overnight.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.470
We can't decriminalize sex work overnight
either.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:28.380
But as we continue to have dialogue with our
representatives.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:35.000
Perhaps we can shift it a little bit closer
to the kind of government that we say we have.

